# PoliLingo — environment variables
#
# Copy to `.env.local` and fill in real values. `.env.local` is gitignored and must
# never be committed.
#
#   Copy-Item .env.example .env.local
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE RULE THAT MATTERS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# In Next.js, any variable named NEXT_PUBLIC_* is compiled into the JavaScript bundle
# and downloaded by every visitor. It is published, permanently, to anyone who opens
# the site. Everything without that prefix is readable only by server-side code.
#
# Getting this wrong with the Supabase service-role key would hand every visitor a
# credential that bypasses Row Level Security on the entire database. The `Secrets`
# CI workflow fails the build if a variable name combines NEXT_PUBLIC_ with
# SERVICE_ROLE, SECRET, PRIVATE_KEY, PASSWORD or _TOKEN.
#
# When you add a variable anywhere, add it here in the same pull request. This file is
# the authoritative list of what the app expects. See docs/configuration.md.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STAGE 0 — today
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The app currently requires NO environment variables. It is fully static plus client
# state, with progress in localStorage. Everything below arrives during Stage 1, and is
# listed now so the contract exists before the first secret does.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# D2 — accounts and progress sync (arrives ~week 4)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Supabase project URL. Safe to publish.
# Dashboard -> Settings -> API -> Project URL
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=

# Supabase anonymous key. Public BY DESIGN — it identifies the project, it does not
# authorise anything on its own. Its safety rests entirely on Row Level Security being
# correct. See docs/technical/data-model.md in the docs repository.
# Dashboard -> Settings -> API -> Project API keys -> anon public
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# Supabase service-role key. BYPASSES ROW LEVEL SECURITY COMPLETELY.
#
# Ideally this never appears here at all. It belongs in the `content` repository's
# release workflow secrets, where the content sync job runs. Only uncomment it if a
# server-side route in this app genuinely needs privileged database access, and say in
# the pull request why.
#
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# D5 — native audio library (arrives ~week 5)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Public base URL for lesson audio, served from the polilingo-audio-pub R2 bucket
# through its custom domain. Safe to publish; it is in every <audio> element anyway.
NEXT_PUBLIC_AUDIO_BASE_URL=

# R2 credentials are NOT set here. The app reads public audio over plain HTTPS and
# needs no S3 credentials at runtime. Writing to R2 happens in the content repository's
# release workflow. If you ever find yourself adding an R2 secret to this app, stop and
# ask why the app is writing to object storage.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# D3, D4 — content (arrives ~week 3)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Which content release this build was compiled against, e.g. content@2026.10.1.
# Set automatically by CI; useful locally for reproducing a specific build.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CONTENT_VERSION=

# Set to `published` in production and `preview` locally to see unreviewed drafts.
# Defaults to `published`.
# CONTENT_CHANNEL=published


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# D10 — analytics (arrives ~week 8)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# There is no third-party analytics key here and there should never be one. ADR-0015
# records the decision to use first-party analytics only: practice events go into our
# own Postgres, with no tag manager, no ad SDK and no third-party script on the learner
# path. If someone proposes adding one, that is an ADR, not an environment variable.
